[x] text of filter tags for channels in the feed view not vertically centered
[x] history is not updating correctly somtimes -> does not contains recently posted items
    -> confirmed: backend history routes (/posts/mine, /posts/reviewed) are correct
       (proper ordering, indexed, no caching). App-side bug: postedHistoryProvider /
       reviewedHistoryProvider are plain (non-autoDispose) providers that cache page 1
       indefinitely and were never invalidated after the mutations that make them stale.
       Fixed by invalidating postedHistoryProvider after create_post succeeds
       (create_post_screen.dart) and reviewedHistoryProvider inside
       FeedNotifier.applyReviewResult (feed_providers.dart), matching the existing
       feedNotifierProvider invalidation convention.
       Follow-up: the history button's counter badge (Profile screen "Posted"/
       "Reviewed" stat tiles) was still stale even after the above fix - it's driven
       by a separate statsProvider (FutureProvider.autoDispose), not the history
       providers. Root cause: AppShell's StatefulShellRoute.indexedStack keeps every
       tab (including Profile) permanently alive, so ProfileScreen's watch on
       statsProvider never lets it autoDispose/refetch - only a full app restart did.
       Fixed by invalidating statsProvider at the same two mutation points. @done
[x] emojis do not render os based -> currently always android style i think
    -> confirmed cause: Flutter web (CanvasKit) rasterizes all text on canvas instead
       of DOM text, so the browser/OS never gets to substitute its native emoji font -
       CanvasKit always uses its bundled Noto (Android-style) emoji regardless of host
       OS. Checked all three alternatives: HTML renderer was fully removed in Flutter
       3.44 (no --web-renderer flag anymore); Skwasm (Wasm-compiled Skia) has the exact
       same bundled-Noto behavior, no emoji improvement, and iOS Safari still falls back
       to JS+CanvasKit anyway; no first-party CanvasKit toggle exists to defer to OS
       emoji - this is an open, unresolved upstream Flutter limitation
       (flutter/flutter#166769), not something we can configure around. Native iOS app
       builds are unaffected (they don't go through CanvasKit). Decided not to build a
       custom DOM-overlay workaround given the effort/fragility vs. payoff - revisit if
       Flutter ships first-party support.
